1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,259
This video shows how to translate a regular expression into an N F A. We use four templates, one for each regex construct.

2
00:00:09,709 --> 00:00:21,591
First we learn the templates. Then we build the N F A for a or a b star, step by step, as the slides do. At the end we solve three questions from the question bank.

3
00:00:22,041 --> 00:00:32,985
We want a program that recognizes the strings of a regular expression. The plan is a small compiler. It goes from a regex to a minimal D F A.

4
00:00:33,435 --> 00:00:43,846
Step 1 translates the regex into an N F A. It uses one template for each construct of the regex. This is the topic of this video.

5
00:00:44,296 --> 00:00:49,651
Step 2 converts the N F A into a D F A, with the subset construction.

6
00:00:50,101 --> 00:00:53,279
Step 3 makes the D F A as small as possible.

7
00:00:53,729 --> 00:01:04,204
Each layer is easy to prove correct. Together they are a reference implementation. It is not the fastest method, but it is fast enough most of the time.

8
00:01:04,654 --> 00:01:24,579
The method is syntax-directed translation. The grammar of the input language guides the translation. Almost every translation in this course is of this kind. Step 0: define a translation for each grammar production. This translation does not depend on a particular input.

9
00:01:25,029 --> 00:01:32,624
Step 1: parse the input. Here the input is the regex a or a b star. The result is this parse tree.

10
00:01:33,074 --> 00:01:47,367
Step 2: apply the translations to the parse tree. Start at the bottom and work up. Each node gets a machine, built from the machines of its children. Each constant at the bottom gets a machine with 2 states.

11
00:01:47,817 --> 00:01:53,428
The star node adds 2 new states around its child. 2 plus 2 is 4 states.

12
00:01:53,878 --> 00:02:00,854
The concatenation node joins its two children. It adds no states. 2 plus 4 is 6 states.

13
00:02:01,304 --> 00:02:07,619
The alternation node at the root adds 2 new states. 2 plus 6 plus 2 is 10 states.

14
00:02:08,069 --> 00:02:14,533
Step 3: the result is the translation of the root. Here it is an N F A with 10 states.

15
00:02:14,983 --> 00:02:21,063
Regular expressions have four constructs, plus the empty string. The empty string is nothing.

16
00:02:21,513 --> 00:02:23,390
A constant is one thing.

17
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:26,763
Concatenation is one thing after another.

18
00:02:27,213 --> 00:02:29,687
Alternation is one thing or another.

19
00:02:30,137 --> 00:02:33,529
Repetition is one thing, zero or more times.

20
00:02:33,979 --> 00:02:39,078
The question mark and the plus are syntactic sugar. They are not new constructs.

21
00:02:39,528 --> 00:02:42,621
x question mark is x or epsilon.

22
00:02:43,071 --> 00:02:53,290
x plus is x x star. Rewrite them before you build the machine. In x x star, x appears two times. Each copy gets its own states.

23
00:02:53,740 --> 00:03:03,404
We need one translation per construct. The translations produce N F As, because we use epsilon edges as glue between the parts.

24
00:03:03,854 --> 00:03:11,171
Template 1 is for a constant. It is one edge between two states. The label of the edge is the constant.

25
00:03:11,621 --> 00:03:13,371
State 1 is the entry.

26
00:03:13,821 --> 00:03:16,423
State 2 is the exit, and it accepts.

27
00:03:16,873 --> 00:03:23,380
The empty string uses the same shape, with an epsilon edge. The course notes draw it this way.

28
00:03:23,830 --> 00:03:34,198
This is the invariant. Every machine that the templates build has exactly one entry state and one exit state. The exit is the accepting state.

29
00:03:34,648 --> 00:03:42,136
Because of this, we can plug any machine into any template as one unit. We draw such a machine as a box M.

30
00:03:42,586 --> 00:03:50,074
Template 2 is concatenation, x y. M x is the machine for x. It may be more than a constant.

31
00:03:50,524 --> 00:03:52,913
M y is the machine for y.

32
00:03:53,363 --> 00:04:02,857
Join the two machines end to end. An epsilon edge goes from the exit of M x, state 2, to the entry of M y, state 3.

33
00:04:03,307 --> 00:04:07,509
The entry of the new machine is the entry of M x, state 1.

34
00:04:07,959 --> 00:04:12,354
The exit of the new machine is the exit of M y, state 4.

35
00:04:12,804 --> 00:04:14,873
State 2 no longer accepts.

36
00:04:15,323 --> 00:04:27,590
Here it is for two constants, x then y. State 2 goes to state 3 on epsilon. This video uses the epsilon edge, as the slides do in the worked example.

37
00:04:28,040 --> 00:04:37,021
You can also merge the two states. The exit of M x becomes the entry of M y. The slides say this is the same thing.

38
00:04:37,471 --> 00:04:40,821
Both machines accept exactly the string x y.

39
00:04:41,271 --> 00:04:47,009
Template 3 is alternation, x or y. Put the two machines side by side.

40
00:04:47,459 --> 00:04:49,678
Add a new entry state, new 1.

41
00:04:50,128 --> 00:04:53,264
Add a new exit state, new 2. It accepts.

42
00:04:53,714 --> 00:05:00,967
From new 1, two epsilon edges go to the entries of M x and M y. The oracle picks the branch.

43
00:05:01,417 --> 00:05:06,836
From the exits of M x and M y, two epsilon edges go to new 2.

44
00:05:07,286 --> 00:05:11,595
So the template adds 2 new states and 4 new epsilon edges.

45
00:05:12,045 --> 00:05:15,629
Here it is for two constants. State 5 is new 1.

46
00:05:16,079 --> 00:05:17,829
State 6 is new 2.

47
00:05:18,279 --> 00:05:22,353
The string x goes through the upper branch: 5, 1, 2, 6.

48
00:05:22,803 --> 00:05:30,163
The string y goes through the lower branch: 5, 3, 4, 6. So the machine accepts x and y.

49
00:05:30,613 --> 00:05:36,075
Template 4 is repetition, x star. It puts a loop around the machine M.

50
00:05:36,525 --> 00:05:39,149
Add two new states, new 1 and new 2.

51
00:05:39,599 --> 00:05:48,260
An epsilon edge goes from new 1 into M, and one goes from the exit of M to new 2. This path runs M one time.

52
00:05:48,710 --> 00:05:56,177
A third epsilon edge goes from new 1 directly to new 2. It skips M. This gives zero copies.

53
00:05:56,627 --> 00:06:02,664
A fourth epsilon edge goes from the exit of M back to its entry. It runs M again.

54
00:06:03,114 --> 00:06:09,173
So again 2 new states and 4 new epsilon edges: skip it, run it, run it again.

55
00:06:09,623 --> 00:06:16,236
Here it is for the constant x. The empty string takes the skip edge, from 3 directly to 4.

56
00:06:16,686 --> 00:06:19,395
The string x goes 3, 1, 2, 4.

57
00:06:19,845 --> 00:06:28,613
The string x x goes 3, 1, 2, then back to 1, then 2 again, then 4. Each trip around the loop reads one more x.

58
00:06:29,063 --> 00:06:34,354
Now the exercise from the slides. Build the N F A for a or a b star.

59
00:06:34,804 --> 00:06:41,332
First, parse the regex. The star binds tightest. Then concatenation, then alternation.

60
00:06:41,782 --> 00:06:47,073
So the tree is: alternation of a, and the concatenation of a with b star.

61
00:06:47,523 --> 00:06:54,285
Build from the bottom up. The three constants come first, from left to right. They get states 1 to 6.

62
00:06:54,735 --> 00:07:02,095
Then each operator node gets its template, from the bottom up. The repetition adds the new states 7 and 8.

63
00:07:02,545 --> 00:07:06,855
The concatenation adds an epsilon edge from 4 to 7.

64
00:07:07,305 --> 00:07:10,569
The alternation adds the new states 9 and 10.

65
00:07:11,019 --> 00:07:16,651
Do not think about whether the result is right. Only match each piece against its template.

66
00:07:17,101 --> 00:07:21,069
Start with the atoms. The first a gets states 1 and 2.

67
00:07:21,519 --> 00:07:23,780
The second a gets states 3 and 4.

68
00:07:24,230 --> 00:07:28,710
The b gets states 5 and 6. These are three constant machines.

69
00:07:29,160 --> 00:07:34,131
Each machine has one entry, with a start arrow: states 1, 3 and 5.

70
00:07:34,581 --> 00:07:43,199
Each machine has one exit, and it accepts: states 2, 4 and 6. So each machine can go into a template as a unit.

71
00:07:43,649 --> 00:07:51,841
Next, the lowest operator: the repetition b star. It uses template 4 around the b machine, states 5 and 6.

72
00:07:52,291 --> 00:07:57,753
Add the new states 7 and 8. Now 7 is the entry and 8 is the exit.

73
00:07:58,203 --> 00:08:01,445
An epsilon edge goes from 7 into 5.

74
00:08:01,895 --> 00:08:04,882
An epsilon edge goes from 6 out to 8.

75
00:08:05,332 --> 00:08:08,127
The skip edge goes from 7 to 8, below.

76
00:08:08,577 --> 00:08:11,457
The back edge goes from 6 to 5, above.

77
00:08:11,907 --> 00:08:20,995
Now the machine for b star has entry 7 and exit 8. Template 4 added 2 new states and 4 new epsilon edges.

78
00:08:21,445 --> 00:08:30,767
Next, the concatenation a b star. It joins the second a machine, states 3 and 4, and the b star machine, states 5 to 8.

79
00:08:31,217 --> 00:08:39,345
Template 2: an epsilon edge glues the exit of the a machine, state 4, to the entry of b star, state 7.

80
00:08:39,795 --> 00:08:43,635
The machine for a b star has entry 3 and exit 8.

81
00:08:44,085 --> 00:08:47,733
Last, the root: the alternation of a and a b star.

82
00:08:48,183 --> 00:08:51,042
Template 3 adds a new entry, state 9.

83
00:08:51,492 --> 00:08:57,380
Two epsilon edges go from 9 to the entries of the two branches, states 1 and 3.

84
00:08:57,830 --> 00:09:00,646
It also adds a new accepting state, 10.

85
00:09:01,096 --> 00:09:05,021
Epsilon edges go to 10 from both exits, 2 and 8.

86
00:09:05,471 --> 00:09:12,127
This is the N F A for a or a b star. Ten states, start state 9, accepting state 10.

87
00:09:12,577 --> 00:09:22,305
The machine is ugly to execute. It has epsilon moves and choices everywhere. For example, state 9 has two epsilon edges out.

88
00:09:22,755 --> 00:09:36,729
But it is easy to check. Each piece matches a template, so it is correct by construction. For example, states 7, 5, 6 and 8 are template 4 around b. A wrong arrow is easy to see.

89
00:09:37,179 --> 00:09:49,445
Test it anyway. The string a b b has a path to 10: 9, 3, 4, 7, 5, 6, back to 5, 6, 8, 10. So the N F A accepts a b b.

90
00:09:49,895 --> 00:10:00,349
The string b a has no path. From 9, the epsilon edges reach only 1 and 3, and neither has a b edge. So the N F A rejects b a.

91
00:10:00,799 --> 00:10:09,033
To run this machine, a program must follow every choice. The next video removes the choices with the subset construction.

92
00:10:09,483 --> 00:10:25,313
One more fact from the slides. a or a b star is the same language as a b star, because a is in a b star. It is hard to argue this directly. The pipeline finds it: it ends with this 2 state machine for a b star.

93
00:10:25,763 --> 00:10:38,904
The templates give a quick check. Each constant, and each epsilon, adds 2 states and 1 edge. In a or a b star, the three constants give states 1 to 6 and the edges a, a and b.

94
00:10:39,354 --> 00:10:45,989
Concatenation adds 1 epsilon edge and no states. Here it is the edge from 4 to 7.

95
00:10:46,439 --> 00:10:54,225
Alternation adds 2 states and 4 epsilon edges. Here they are states 9 and 10, with their 4 edges.

96
00:10:54,675 --> 00:11:02,718
Repetition also adds 2 states and 4 epsilon edges. Here they are states 7 and 8, with their 4 edges.

97
00:11:03,168 --> 00:11:10,571
So a or a b star has 3 constants, 1 concatenation, 1 alternation and 1 repetition.

98
00:11:11,021 --> 00:11:20,343
The states: 2 times the sum of 3 constants, 1 alternation and 1 repetition. That is 2 times 5, which is 10 states.

99
00:11:20,793 --> 00:11:28,985
The edges: 3 plus 1 plus 4 plus 4, which is 12 edges. The drawing has 10 states and 12 edges.

100
00:11:29,435 --> 00:11:37,819
On the exam, use this count to check your drawing. With merged concatenation, subtract 1 state per concatenation.

101
00:11:38,269 --> 00:11:48,531
Now the question bank. Question 4.3.1. Draw an N F A for R question mark, which matches zero or one copy of R.

102
00:11:48,981 --> 00:11:53,781
Then draw one for R plus, which matches one or more copies of R.

103
00:11:54,231 --> 00:12:00,737
The bank gives no figure, so this is our solution. Hint: use the rewrite rules from the slides.

104
00:12:01,187 --> 00:12:02,830
Pause the video and try it.

105
00:12:03,280 --> 00:12:07,184
Part a. Rewrite R question mark as R or epsilon.

106
00:12:07,634 --> 00:12:13,586
The box is the machine for R. Its entry is state 1, and its exit is state 2.

107
00:12:14,036 --> 00:12:20,415
Epsilon is a constant. Its machine is two states with an epsilon edge, 3 to 4.

108
00:12:20,865 --> 00:12:28,630
Now apply template 3, alternation. New state 5 is the entry, with epsilon edges to 1 and 3.

109
00:12:29,080 --> 00:12:33,752
New state 6 is the exit, with epsilon edges from 2 and 4.

110
00:12:34,202 --> 00:12:40,389
The lower path, 5, 3, 4, 6, reads nothing. So it gives zero copies of R.

111
00:12:40,839 --> 00:12:48,689
Check with R equal to a. Put the edge 1, a, 2 into the box. The machine has 6 states and 6 edges.

112
00:12:49,139 --> 00:12:58,505
It accepts the empty string on the path 5, 3, 4, 6. It accepts a on the path 5, 1, 2, 6. It accepts nothing else.

113
00:12:58,955 --> 00:13:06,549
With R equal to a b, it accepts the empty string and a b only. A python check tested both cases.

114
00:13:06,999 --> 00:13:12,290
Part b. Rewrite R plus as R R star. It has two copies of R.

115
00:13:12,740 --> 00:13:17,455
The first R is a plain copy of the machine for R, states 1 and 2.

116
00:13:17,905 --> 00:13:21,297
The second R is a new copy, states 3 and 4.

117
00:13:21,747 --> 00:13:26,120
Put template 4 around the copy. Add the new states 5 and 6.

118
00:13:26,570 --> 00:13:31,093
Add the epsilon edges from 5 to 3, and from 4 to 6.

119
00:13:31,543 --> 00:13:34,380
Add the skip edge from 5 to 6, above.

120
00:13:34,830 --> 00:13:37,027
Add the back edge from 4 to 3.

121
00:13:37,477 --> 00:13:42,469
Template 2 joins the two parts: an epsilon edge from 2 to 5.

122
00:13:42,919 --> 00:13:49,149
A string must go through the first copy, from 1 to 2. So it has at least one copy of R.

123
00:13:49,599 --> 00:13:54,484
Check with R equal to a. The machine has 6 states and 7 edges.

124
00:13:54,934 --> 00:13:58,027
The string a takes the path 1, 2, 5, 6.

125
00:13:58,477 --> 00:14:02,595
The string a a takes the path 1, 2, 5, 3, 4, 6.

126
00:14:03,045 --> 00:14:13,839
The empty string has no path, because state 1 has only the a edge. A python check tested R equal to a and R equal to a b. Both are correct.

127
00:14:14,289 --> 00:14:25,404
Question 4.3.2. Draw an N F A for a or b, star, then a, then a or b, cubed. The bank gives no figure, so this is our solution.

128
00:14:25,854 --> 00:14:27,497
Pause the video and try it.

129
00:14:27,947 --> 00:14:34,667
The power 3 is not a new construct. It means three copies: a or b, a or b, a or b.

130
00:14:35,117 --> 00:14:39,938
So the regex is a chain of five parts, joined by concatenation.

131
00:14:40,388 --> 00:14:50,799
Count first, part by part. a or b, star, has 2 constants, 1 alternation and 1 repetition. That is 2 times 4, which is 8 states.

132
00:14:51,249 --> 00:14:53,190
The constant a has 2 states.

133
00:14:53,640 --> 00:15:00,637
Each a or b has 2 constants and 1 alternation. That is 2 times 3, which is 6 states.

134
00:15:01,087 --> 00:15:13,589
The total is 8 plus 2 plus 6 plus 6 plus 6, which is 28 states. It is also 2 times the sum of 9 constants, 4 alternations and 1 repetition.

135
00:15:14,039 --> 00:15:20,951
Build it part by part. First the inner a or b. The constants a and b get states 1 to 4.

136
00:15:21,401 --> 00:15:27,097
The alternation adds the new states 19 and 20, with four epsilon edges.

137
00:15:27,547 --> 00:15:37,915
The repetition around it adds 21 and 22, with the skip edge above and the back edge from 20 to 19. State 21 is the start.

138
00:15:38,365 --> 00:15:45,255
Then the constant a, states 5 and 6. An epsilon edge from 22 to 5 glues it on.

139
00:15:45,705 --> 00:15:56,735
Then the first copy of a or b: states 7 to 10, and the new states 23 and 24. An epsilon edge from 6 to 23 glues it on.

140
00:15:57,185 --> 00:16:08,086
The second copy works the same way: states 11 to 14, and 25 and 26. An epsilon edge from 24 to 25 glues it on.

141
00:16:08,536 --> 00:16:18,349
The third copy: states 15 to 18, and 27 and 28. An epsilon edge from 26 to 27 glues it on.

142
00:16:18,799 --> 00:16:32,026
State 28 is the only accepting state. That is 28 states, as the count said. A python check ran this machine on every string up to length 9. It agrees with the regex.

143
00:16:32,476 --> 00:16:41,585
The question asks for any N F A. The templates always give a correct one. A smaller N F A is also correct, if you check it.

144
00:16:42,035 --> 00:16:44,297
Here state 0 loops on a and b.

145
00:16:44,747 --> 00:16:47,157
Then an a edge goes from 0 to 1.

146
00:16:47,607 --> 00:16:53,431
Then exactly three more letters take the machine from 1 to 4. State 4 accepts.

147
00:16:53,881 --> 00:16:59,129
Run the string b a a b a. State 0 reads b and stays in 0.

148
00:16:59,579 --> 00:17:02,054
On the first a, the path goes to 1.

149
00:17:02,504 --> 00:17:04,360
The next a takes it to 2.

150
00:17:04,810 --> 00:17:06,666
The b takes it to 3.

151
00:17:07,116 --> 00:17:10,679
The last a takes it to 4. State 4 accepts.

152
00:17:11,129 --> 00:17:21,475
From state 0, the letter a has two edges, to 0 and to 1. This is the nondeterminism. The oracle guesses the a that is fourth from the end.

153
00:17:21,925 --> 00:17:40,272
This machine has 5 states. A python check compared it with the regex and with the 28 state machine, on every string up to length 11. If you draw a small machine by hand, test it like this. The templates need no test, because each piece matches a template.

154
00:17:40,722 --> 00:17:49,746
Question 4.3.3. Construct an N F A for this regex. The bank gives no figure, so this is our solution.

155
00:17:50,196 --> 00:17:51,839
Pause the video and try it.

156
00:17:52,289 --> 00:17:59,862
A large regex is easier if you give names to its parts. Call a or b, b, b star the part X.

157
00:18:00,312 --> 00:18:02,296
Call a a star the part Z.

158
00:18:02,746 --> 00:18:05,477
Call a or b, a, b the part Y.

159
00:18:05,927 --> 00:18:12,732
Then the regex is X star or Z, followed by Y star. This is the top of the parse tree.

160
00:18:13,182 --> 00:18:20,478
Build the part X first. a or b uses the alternation template. The constants get states 1 to 4.

161
00:18:20,928 --> 00:18:24,192
The alternation adds the new states 9 and 10.

162
00:18:24,642 --> 00:18:31,127
Then the constant b, states 5 and 6. An epsilon edge from 10 to 5 joins it.

163
00:18:31,577 --> 00:18:35,353
Then b star. The constant b gets states 7 and 8.

164
00:18:35,803 --> 00:18:42,971
Template 4 adds the new states 11 and 12. An epsilon edge from 6 to 11 joins it.

165
00:18:43,421 --> 00:18:56,797
The part X has 12 states: 2 times the sum of 4 constants, 1 alternation and 1 repetition. The numbers here are local to this part. In the whole machine, the states get other numbers.

166
00:18:57,247 --> 00:19:02,986
Now put the parts together, from the bottom of the tree up. The part X has 12 states.

167
00:19:03,436 --> 00:19:10,604
Template 4 around X gives X star. It adds 2 new states. 12 plus 2 is 14 states.

168
00:19:11,054 --> 00:19:18,329
Z is a a star: 2 constants and 1 repetition. So Z has 2 times 3, which is 6 states.

169
00:19:18,779 --> 00:19:24,944
The alternation of X star and Z adds a new entry and a new exit: 2 more states.

170
00:19:25,394 --> 00:19:34,141
Y is a or b, a, b: 4 constants and 1 alternation. So Y has 2 times 5, which is 10 states.

171
00:19:34,591 --> 00:19:39,753
Template 4 around Y gives Y star. 10 plus 2 is 12 states.

172
00:19:40,203 --> 00:19:46,326
Next, an epsilon edge glues the exit of the alternation to the entry of Y star.

173
00:19:46,776 --> 00:19:50,232
The exit of Y star is the only accepting state.

174
00:19:50,682 --> 00:20:04,783
The total is 14 plus 6 plus 2 plus 12, which is 34 states. The count rule gives the same: 2 times the sum of 10 constants, 3 alternations and 4 repetitions.

175
00:20:05,233 --> 00:20:13,319
A python build of the templates also gives 34 states. It matches the regex on every string up to length 8.

176
00:20:13,769 --> 00:20:23,219
Thirty four states is a lot to draw in an exam. Here is a smaller N F A, our solution, made by hand. State 1 is the start.

177
00:20:23,669 --> 00:20:26,400
State 6 is the only accepting state.

178
00:20:26,850 --> 00:20:32,141
The cycle 2, 3, 4 is X star. From 2, a or b goes to 3.

179
00:20:32,591 --> 00:20:37,177
Then b goes to 4, and 4 loops on b. That is b, b star.

180
00:20:37,627 --> 00:20:41,638
The epsilon edge from 4 back to 2 starts the next X.

181
00:20:42,088 --> 00:20:50,771
The epsilon edges from 1 to 2, and from 2 to 6, enter and leave X star. So zero copies of X are allowed.

182
00:20:51,221 --> 00:20:59,263
The a edge from 1 to 5, and the a loop on 5, make a a star. The epsilon edge from 5 to 6 leaves Z.

183
00:20:59,713 --> 00:21:05,559
No edge goes into state 1. So after an X, the machine cannot continue with Z.

184
00:21:06,009 --> 00:21:11,619
The cycle 6, 7, 8 is Y star: a or b, then a, then b, back to 6.

185
00:21:12,069 --> 00:21:16,123
State 6 accepts, so zero copies of Y are allowed.

186
00:21:16,573 --> 00:21:28,114
This machine has 8 states. A python check compared it with the regex and with the 34 state template machine, on every string up to length 11. They agree.

187
00:21:28,564 --> 00:21:40,020
To summarize. Regex to N F A is a syntax-directed translation. Parse the regex, then apply one template per node of the tree, from the bottom up.

188
00:21:40,470 --> 00:21:47,403
Rewrite the sugar first. x question mark is x or epsilon. x plus is x x star.

189
00:21:47,853 --> 00:21:55,853
A constant is two states with one edge. Every machine has one entry and one exit, so the machines plug together.

190
00:21:56,303 --> 00:22:07,013
Concatenation adds one epsilon edge, or merges two states. Alternation and repetition each add 2 new states and 4 new epsilon edges.

191
00:22:07,463 --> 00:22:23,271
Check your work. Count 2 states for each constant, each alternation and each repetition. If you draw a smaller machine by hand, test it against the regex. Next video: N F A to D F A, the subset construction.
